steps.message:
I wrote `/app/bin/summarize.py` and ran it on `/app/data`. The output is in `/app/out/summary.json`, and `check_summary.py` says every value matches the reference figures. That check only covers the current files, so it can't confirm the script will be right on other exports.

The script uses only the standard library and follows the rules in the docs rather than anything specific to the current data. It runs in well under a second here. It handles:
- all eight depot formats, including gzip-compressed files, byte order marks, `# charset:` directives, and fixed-width layouts that switch by line length;
- directives and header rows that appear part way through a file;
- weights that carry their own unit, with the unit written in the row;
- ship times with UTC offsets, which are converted to UTC before the month is taken;
- applying each depot's exports in `seq` order, with corrections, voids, resends and handovers resolved as the docs describe.

One choice of mine: a void record counts toward `corrections_applied` even if the depot has no current record for that number. The docs say a void always follows a new record, so I couldn't test that case.

I also wrote `/app/STATUS.json` with status `success`.
